-# The DevTools Times
+# smallpt · The browser edition
 
-This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
+Kevin Beason’s [smallpt](https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/), compiled from C++ to WebAssembly using Emscripten. The original source and MIT license are preserved in `renderer/`.
 
+## Run
 
 ```sh
-pnpm install && pnpm run start
+pnpm install
+pnpm dev --host 0.0.0.0
 ```
 
-The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
+Open `/` or `/index.html`. Rendering happens locally in a module worker. Randomize both sphere colors, edit them individually, adjust samples and resolution, restore the original scene, or download a PNG.
 
-## Tech Stack
+## Rebuild WebAssembly
 
-- **[Astro](https://astro.build/)**: The web framework for building content-driven websites.
-- **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
-- **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
-- **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+With Emscripten installed:
+
+```sh
+pnpm build:wasm
+node scripts/check-renderer.mjs
+pnpm build
+```
+
+The compiled module is committed under `public/renderer/`; Emscripten is not needed to run or build the site. The adapter exports initialization, row rendering, and an RGBA framebuffer instead of writing a PPM file. The original radiance algorithm, Cornell box geometry, 2×2 subpixel sampling, and mirror/glass materials are retained. OpenMP is replaced by a dedicated browser worker, with row updates for progress and cancellation by terminating the worker.
+
+`preview.png` is a 128 spp render generated from this same C++ adapter; it displays immediately while the browser renders the initial scene. No browser verification was performed.
